WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.327
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Grammar, level B one.

2
00:00:05.627 --> 00:00:09.071
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.371 --> 00:00:16.169
Unit sixteen: Conditional Sentences Review. Today you will choose between zero, first and second conditionals.

4
00:00:16.469 --> 00:00:19.144
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:23.379
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.217 --> 00:00:25.682
Zero conditional.

7
00:00:26.683 --> 00:00:30.993
Zero conditional means if + present, present: general truths.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:33.560
If you heat ice, it melts.

9
00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:37.336
Your turn. Say: Zero conditional.

10
00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:41.700
Zero conditional.

11
00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.648
First conditional.

12
00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:48.414
First conditional means if + present, will: real future.

13
00:00:49.017 --> 00:00:50.621
If it rains, I will stay.

14
00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:53.962
Your turn. Say: First conditional.

15
00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.115
First conditional.

16
00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:00.095
Second conditional.

17
00:01:01.083 --> 00:01:04.755
Second conditional means if + past, would: imaginary.

18
00:01:05.350 --> 00:01:06.966
If I won, I would travel.

19
00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:10.435
Your turn. Say: Second conditional.

20
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:14.800
Second conditional.

21
00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.679
If clause.

22
00:01:17.683 --> 00:01:20.194
If clause means the part that gives the condition.

23
00:01:20.783 --> 00:01:22.469
The if clause comes first.

24
00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:25.643
Your turn. Say: If clause.

25
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:29.873
If clause.

26
00:01:30.617 --> 00:01:32.001
Result clause.

27
00:01:33.017 --> 00:01:35.550
Result clause means the part that gives the result.

28
00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:37.929
The result clause has will.

29
00:01:38.517 --> 00:01:41.006
Your turn. Say: Result clause.

30
00:01:43.867 --> 00:01:45.204
Result clause.

31
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:46.730
Real.

32
00:01:47.717 --> 00:01:49.658
Real means true or possible.

33
00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:51.704
A real situation.

34
00:01:52.317 --> 00:01:55.642
Your turn. Say: Real.

35
00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.153
Real.

36
00:01:59.917 --> 00:02:00.848
Unreal.

37
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:03.606
Unreal means imagined.

38
00:02:04.217 --> 00:02:06.147
An unreal situation.

39
00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.170
Your turn. Say: Unreal.

40
00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:13.174
Unreal.

41
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:14.558
Type.

42
00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:17.584
Type means a kind or category.

43
00:02:18.183 --> 00:02:19.637
Which type is it?

44
00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:22.902
Your turn. Say: Type.

45
00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:26.489
Type.

46
00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.904
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.237
Visal, how do you choose the right conditional?

48
00:02:35.350 --> 00:02:46.315
I ask whether it is a general truth, a real future possibility, or an imaginary situation. The type depends on how real the situation is.

49
00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:49.172
Give me the three patterns.

50
00:02:50.283 --> 00:03:04.284
Zero: if + present, present. First: if + present, will. Second: if + past, would. The tense in the if clause moves back as the situation becomes less real.

51
00:03:05.383 --> 00:03:06.837
Which one for a fact?

52
00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:21.028
Zero, for facts and rules: If you mix red and blue, you get purple. First is for plans and warnings, and second is for dreams and advice, like if I were you.

53
00:03:22.167 --> 00:03:23.702
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.550 --> 00:03:28.465
Grammar focus: Conditionals: zero, first and second.

55
00:03:28.765 --> 00:03:29.882
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.583 --> 00:03:32.792
If you heat ice, it melts.

57
00:03:33.683 --> 00:03:35.602
If it rains, I will stay home.

58
00:03:36.517 --> 00:03:38.865
If I won, I would travel the world.

59
00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:48.773
Zero = facts. First = real future. Second = imaginary. The if-clause tense moves back as the situation becomes less real.

60
00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:53.051
Be careful! Don't say: If I will see her, I tell her.

61
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:58.195
Say: If I see her, I will tell her.

62
00:03:58.495 --> 00:04:00.065
Use the present after if.

63
00:04:00.867 --> 00:04:03.531
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:05.532
Which Conditional?.

65
00:04:05.832 --> 00:04:09.413
If you heat ice, it melts. That is a general truth.

66
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:13.201
If it rains tomorrow, we will stay at home.

67
00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:16.954
If I won the lottery, I would travel the world.

68
00:04:17.967 --> 00:04:22.102
The if clause gives the condition, and the result clause gives the result.

69
00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.831
The type depends on how real the situation is.

70
00:04:26.933 --> 00:04:30.784
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:31.650 --> 00:04:34.195
If you mix red and blue, you blank purple.

72
00:04:34.495 --> 00:04:38.358
A, get. B, will got. C, would get.

73
00:04:41.900 --> 00:04:43.458
The answer is A: get.

74
00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:46.489
If it rains, we blank stay.

75
00:04:46.789 --> 00:04:50.187
A, would. B, will. C, stayed.

76
00:04:53.733 --> 00:04:55.803
The answer is B: will.

77
00:04:56.950 --> 00:04:59.228
If I blank rich, I would help people.

78
00:04:59.528 --> 00:05:02.334
A, am. B, were. C, will be.

79
00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:07.702
The answer is B: were.

80
00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.513
Which is the second conditional?

81
00:05:10.813 --> 00:05:17.729
A, If I had wings, I would fly.. B, If I have time, I will go.. C, If you boil water, it boils..

82
00:05:21.267 --> 00:05:24.776
The answer is A: If I had wings, I would fly..

83
00:05:25.917 --> 00:05:29.089
According to the reading, what does the type depend on?

84
00:05:29.389 --> 00:05:33.832
A, how real it is. B, the weather. C, the speaker's age.

85
00:05:37.367 --> 00:05:39.947
The answer is A: how real it is.

86
00:05:41.133 --> 00:05:44.041
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:44.883 --> 00:05:49.251
Write one example each of a zero, first, and second conditional sentence.

88
00:05:58.050 --> 00:05:59.515
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:05:59.815 --> 00:06:11.201
Zero: If you heat water to one hundred degrees, it boils. First: If it rains, I will take a taxi. Second: If I had a car, I would drive to the beach.

90
00:06:11.501 --> 00:06:13.303
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:14.217 --> 00:06:19.284
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and conditionals: zero, first and second.

92
00:06:19.584 --> 00:06:24.303
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

93
00:06:24.603 --> 00:06:27.418
See you in Unit seventeen. Goodbye!

94
00:06:27.718 --> 00:06:30.138
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

